      "math_academy_id": "q-142436",
      "knowledge_point_id": "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/c7fd7d815bd006c27be4fc793e4cdc8c66ccd119d86167ad7f7cb1da64a4f2bd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
