  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566",
    "id": "q-141051",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-141051",
      "knowledge_point_id": "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/6c07657992f5c4f708cd7546902c5e26206bfa41f291a68ec7b582cb18a167fe.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a-b.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2)+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na-b=2-(-2)=4.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
